<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astana Cemetery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd–8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Astana Graves</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> Cemetery is an ancient burial ground near the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its tombs, used from about the 3rd to the 8th century, have preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, figurines, paintings and thousands of paper documents, many reused to make burial garments. The documents are a key source for life and trade in the Gaochang kingdom and under the Tang.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The cemetery lies 37 kilometres southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and 6 kilometres from the ancient city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>. It covers 10 square kilometres and holds over 1,000 tombs, mainly of the descendants of Chinese settlers in Gaochang. A sloping passage leads down 4 or 5 metres to a rock-cut entrance, and a step leads into a brick-lined chamber two to four metres wide and up to two metres high. Some tombs have narrow antechambers with niches for figures of guardian beasts, with animal features and sometimes human faces. The dry climate has preserved much, including natural mummies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The dead were wrapped in textiles, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> eye-mask and a piece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, or sometimes coins, over the face. Coins placed in the mouth included Byzantine pieces, local copies of Byzantine and Chinese coins, and Chinese coins; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> compared the custom with the Greek fare for the ferryman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>, while Chinese scholars point to Chinese burials of the first millennium BCE. Coffins held personal objects and paper and silk models of shoes and hats, and food stood near the head: grapes, plums, pears, meat, wheat and pastries, among them a jam tart.</p><p>Believing the afterlife much like this life, the families buried models of people, animals, clothes and musical instruments, listed on a written inventory. Many tombs held epitaph tablets and a banner showing Fuxi and Nüwa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Expeditions sent to Chinese Central Asia early in the 20th century visited the site, among them those of Ōtani Kōzui in 1902, Albert Grünwedel, Albert von Le Coq and Sergey Oldenburg; Stein came in 1907 and again in December 1914. Their finds went to collections in St Petersburg, Tokyo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and Stein's to the British Library, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and the National Museum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. Since 1959 Chinese archaeologists have excavated 456 tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, 205 of them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> fragments, most from paper shoes, hats, belts and coffins made of discarded documents. The two thousand documents recovered give insight into the life of the people there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Astana Cemetery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Documents from the <b>Astana cemetery</b> record contracts, taxes and caravans trading silk, horses and slaves.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaochang-kingdom">Gaochang Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
